# HEC-Vortex Rainfall Preprocessor

**From a spatial design-storm raster to time-stamped precipitation grids for HEC-RAS Rain-on-Grid modelling.**

`Python` · `GeoTIFF` · `HEC-Vortex` · `Rain-on-Grid` · `Hydroinformatics`

## Why this tool?

**HEC-Vortex imports precipitation grids into HEC-DSS, but it does not construct a design storm's temporal distribution from a total-depth raster.**

This preprocessor fills that step. It transforms a **total event rainfall raster** into a sequence of **time-stamped GeoTIFF grids**, which can then be imported into **HEC-Vortex** and converted to **HEC-DSS** for HEC-RAS 2D.

> **Important:** The preprocessor generates GeoTIFF time steps. **HEC-Vortex**, not this application, performs the subsequent DSS import/conversion.

## Main capabilities

- Generate temporal rainfall distributions using **seven methods**:
  - SCS/NRCS Types **I, IA, II and III**
  - **Alternating Blocks** from an IDF relationship
  - **Triangular** hyetograph
  - **Observed** rainfall hyetograph
- Convert rainfall depth into per-time-step, georeferenced grids.
- Clip/mask by catchment boundary, with controlled treatment of missing raster values.
- Preserve the source raster's spatial reference and affine transform across outputs.
- Preview the resulting hyetograph and cumulative rainfall curve.
- Apply mass-conservation checks when writing rainfall grids.

## Workflow

```text
Total rainfall GeoTIFF + catchment boundary
                    |
        Rainfall Preprocessor
       (temporal distribution)
                    |
   Time-stamped GeoTIFF rainfall grids
                    |
              HEC-Vortex
                    |
                HEC-DSS
                    |
        HEC-RAS 2D Rain-on-Grid
```

## Quality controls

- Monotonic cumulative-rainfall interpolation with **PCHIP** when applicable.
- Temporal fractions normalized to conserve total precipitation depth.
- Post-write raster check, with a documented warning threshold of **0.01 mm** for maximum depth difference.
- Checks for georeferencing consistency and unresolved missing data.

## Example from the case study

A documented demonstration used a **24-hour** design storm with a **5-minute** time step, generating **288 rainfall grids** for subsequent HEC-Vortex import.

## Verification and limitations

- The spatial and temporal preprocessing workflow was documented and exercised on case-study data.
- A **quantitative end-to-end check of the rainfall actually imported into DSS** was still identified as a follow-up validation task in the report.
- For SCS Types **I and III**, exact numerical agreement with official fine-resolution reference tables was not independently established; additional verification is needed before regulatory use.
- Synthetic rainfall distributions are not automatically representative of the local rainfall climate. Appropriate parameter selection and validation remain the user's responsibility.

